iMac 18,3 CS8409/CS42L83: make headset capture work, follow the jack live, and
give both microphones a usable level.

Applies to the snd_hda_macbookpro DKMS driver (davidjo/snd_hda_macbookpro),
against the tree at commit be90113. Every part below was confirmed on hardware
on an iMac18,3 running kernel 7.1.9-arch1-2.

Four related fixes, all on the capture path. Deliberately no headset-button
code: that was attempted separately, made the audio crackle and jammed the
codec's interrupt handler, and was removed.

1. Capture follows the jack.
   The driver sets up one microphone and records from the other. On plug-in
   cs_8409_pcm_capture_pre_prepare_hook branches on have_mike and calls
   cs_8409_headcapture_setup, routing the headset mike (pin 0x3c) to the
   cs42l83 ADC 0x1a -- but the PCM stays bound to intmike_adc_nid (0x23 on
   iMacs). Nothing feeds 0x23, so capture is digital silence: peak 0.00000
   with a headset in, while the internal mike alone worked fine.
   Proof: vendor coef 0x82 read back 0x0000 mid-capture (DMIC2_SCL_EN never
   set) and pin 0x45 stayed Pin-ctls 0x00, while coef 0x09 showed the iMac
   model config was correct -- the path was skipped, not misconfigured.
   Fix: in cs_8409_capture_pcm_prepare pick the ADC the hook is about to
   configure and move hinfo->nid there. It must happen BEFORE the hook, since
   cs_8409_store_stream_format caches the DMA stream tag against hinfo->nid
   and cs_8409_really_update_stream_format(0x1a,...) would otherwise find an
   empty cache. It must also switch both ways: hinfo is persistent for the
   life of the codec, so a one-way switch strands the PCM on 0x1a and breaks
   the internal mike for every recording after the first unplug.

2. Plugging or unplugging while capturing.
   The driver's own comment said it: "NOTA BENE - no concept/implementation of
   plugging in while capturing!!", and the handler logged
   "PLUGIN WHILE CAPTURING UNIMPLEMENTED!!" and did nothing. Since the setup
   above only runs at stream start, any application holding the microphone
   open across a jack change kept the old routing forever. OBS does exactly
   that, which made the microphone appear to work and then die permanently the
   first time the jack was touched.
   Fix: cs_8409_capture_follow_jack() re-runs the selection, the setup and
   snd_hda_codec_setup_stream() for the new state, called from the have_mike
   block in cs_8409_plugin_event_continued and from the end of
   cs_8409_unplug_handle_disconnect. The capture hinfo, stream tag and format
   are saved in the spec at prepare time so the handler can rebuild the stream.
   These run from the HDA unsol work item, i.e. process context -- the
   surrounding code already msleeps and does long i2c sequences -- so the call
   is direct rather than deferred. spec->block_unsol is saved and restored
   around it because cs_8409_capture_real clears it on exit and we are nested
   inside an unsol response.
   Verified: 90 s capture held open across a physical unplug and replug. The
   driver logged "capture nid 0x1a -> 0x23 (jack 0 mike 0)" then
   "0x23 -> 0x1a (jack 1 mike 1)", the converters swapped in
   /proc/asound/card0/codec#0 to match, and not one 2-second window lost audio.

3. Internal mike gain -- let the mixer own it.
   cs_8409_intmike_volume_setup was called with a hardcoded 0x27 from four
   sites. Node 0x23's amp caps are ofs=0x33, nsteps=0x3f, stepsize=0x03, so
   0x33 is 0 dB and 0x27 is -12 dB -- while the ALSA control simultaneously
   reported 63 [100%] [12.00dB]. A 24 dB lie, and the driver stamped its value
   over the user's on every capture setup.
   Fix: once the mixer exists, read the value ALSA holds and write that, so the
   control genuinely drives the amp. Before the mixer is built (boot setup runs
   during codec init) the new intmike_gain parameter seeds it, default 0x33.
   Measured: -51.0 dBFS RMS before, -22.9 dBFS after, same room, same capture.
   Note nid 0x45's amp range is only 0..2, so it keeps the original constant
   through a clamp -- passing 0x27 there latches 3, which is out of range, and
   WirePlumber then fails "Failed to set volume of 'Internal Mic Boost'" and
   drops the whole analog device.

4. Headset mike gain.
   The HDA widget for the headset ADC has no Amp-In at all, so ALSA exposes no
   control and the user cannot turn it up. At the codec's reset values the
   earbud mike measured 35x quieter than the internal one: peak 0.0253 versus
   0.905, a 31 dB shortfall.
   All the gain lives in the cs42l83, per CS42L42 DS1083F8 sections 4.1 and
   7.11.3 (same silicon family; mainline sound/soc/codecs/cs42l42.c agrees on
   the register map): 0x1D01 bit 0 ADC_DIG_BOOST is +20 dB, reset 0; 0x1D03
   ADC_VOL is two's complement in 1.0 dB steps, +12 dB (0x0C) down to -96 dB.
   That is +32 dB total, almost exactly the measured deficit.
   macOS does NOT do this -- davidjo's trace of
   AppleHDATDM_CS42L83::enableExtMicrophone shows OSX writing 0x1d03 = 0x00 and
   never touching 0x1d01. Its loudness comes from CoreAudio applying gain in
   software, which has no register equivalent. Going beyond what OSX writes is
   therefore correct here, not a workaround.
   Defaults: boost on plus the full +12 dB trim. With the owner speaking that
   put his voice at -21.7 dBFS with peaks at -8.9 dBFS and no clipped samples.
   Both stages are applied after the ADC, so this is pure digital gain -- it
   lifts the noise floor with the signal and does not improve SNR (measured
   28 dB either way, which belongs to the earbud mike and Apple's analogue
   path). Do not raise the trim further: at +32 dB a plosive close to the mike
   reaches 0 dBFS and clips.

Known remaining issue: a loud high-pitched artefact is audible on the headset
output at the moment of plug-in. Not diagnosed. Most likely HSBIAS being
asserted abruptly or the output SRC not locking cleanly.

